  "strategy": "<h4>Approach and Rationale</h4>\nOur approach combines: \n1. <font color=blue>Grid refinement</font> - Trying multiple resolution grid sizes (128\u2192256\u2192300) to evolve solutions\n2. <font color=blue>Adaptive sampling</font> - Using GPU-accelerated GPU Monte Carlo for efficient sampling\n3. <font color=blue>Neural network</font> - Approximating fitness surfaces for major jumps\n4. <font color=blue>Local optimization</font> - Refining promising candidates\n\nThe key innovations are:\n- Using autoregressive potentials to initialize searches\n- Exploiting hardware acceleration for sampling\n- Adopting multi-resolution strategy to overcome flat optimization landscapes\n\nThe main limitations are:\n- Requires significant memory (300-grid uses 240 variables)\n- Cannot exploit cross-iteration caching due to nonlinear dynamics\n- Constrained by finite time horizon\n\n<h4>Mathematical Specification</h4>\nVariables: \n\u208b Define parameterization (i) = 0.5 * (1 + tanh(a * cos(2\u03c0i/n) + b * sin(2\u03c0i/n) + c\u1d62))\n\u208b Dimensions: n \u2208 {128,256,300}, c \u2208 R\u207f\n\u208b Constraints: 0 \u2264 (i) \u2264 1, \u2211 = n/2\n\nObjective:\n\u208b C\u2085(h,n) = (2.0/n) * max_{k} \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\u208b Computed via: max_corr * dx, where dx=2.0/n, corr = np.correlate(h,1-h,'full')\n\nRepresentation:\n\u208b Parameter optimized via:  h(i) \u2248 proj_{0,1}[tanh(w\u2080 cos(2\u03c0i/n) + w\u2081 sin(2\u03c0i/n))]\n\n<h4>Implementation Procedure</h4>\n<p1>Validate_input(h,n):</p1>\n\u208b Input: NumPy array h of length n, current grid size\n\u208b Output: Projected array to [0,1] with exact sum n/2\n\u208b Steps:\n    1. Clip h to [0,1]\n    2. Adjust values to get exact sum n/2\n    3. Return validated array\n\n<p1>Create_periodic_base(n):</p1>\n\u208b Input: Number of points n\n\u208b Output: Periodic sin-cos base array of length n\n\u208b Steps:\n    1. Generate angles = 2\u03c0i/n for i=0 to n-1\n    2. Return matrix [cos(angles), sin(angles)] for n rows\n\n<git>Optimize_grid(n,seed):</git>\n\u208b Input: Target grid size n, random seed\n\u208b Output: Best found solution for grid n\n\u208b Steps:\n    1. If n=128: Use provided parent\n    2. Else: Generate initial population C \u2208 \u03a6(A,U)\n    3. Initialize highest frequency pattern\n    4. Set running best = uniform candidate\n    5. For t=1 to grid_size_bounds:\n          - Generate sample f form \u2248 proj[ x, sin(2\u03c0i/256) ]\n          - Calculate C\u2085(f) using finite differences\n          - If C\u2085 better than time average, update running best\n          - Prune candidates based on interference pattern\n    6. Return best found\n\n<p1>Upscale_solution(fine_sol, coarse_sol):</p1>\n\u208b Input: Base solution and its refinement\n\u208b Output: Upsampled solution to higher resolution\n\u208b Steps:\n    1. Generate sparse grid interpolation\n    2. Apply frequency-lifting transformation\n    3. Maintain period consistency\n\n<p1>Run(seed,budget):</p1>\n\u208b Input: Python seed, time budget\n\u208b Output: (best_h, best_c5, n_used)\n\u208b Steps:\n    1. Initialize global best to INF\n    2. Precompute initial configuration\n    3. Set parameter frequencies: {128\u2192256:1.0, 128\u2192300:0.8}\n    4. Allocate budget: [800s,250s,50s]\n    5. Optimization loop for grids [128,256,300]:\n        - Reset clock\n        - Compute coarse-to-fine mapping\n        - Set up GPU batches\n        - Run Monte Carlo with bounds projection\n    6. Compare grid results and keep best found\n    7. Return solution with <0.38085 as proof\n\n<h4>Search Choices and Useful Variations</h4>\nThese strategies address:\n1. <font color=green>Exploration-Exploitation</font>\n   \u22b2 Cycle through periodic patterns\n   \u22b2 Vary phase in tanh modulation\n   \u22b2 Use autocorrelation pruning\n\n2. <font color=green>Interpolation</font>\n   \u22b2 Linear vs exponential upsampling\n   \u22b2 Maintain frequency response\n\n3. <font color=green>Hybridization</font>\n   \u22b2 Feed neural patterns into SLSQP\n   \u22b2 Use gradient descent with spectral cutoff\n   \u22b2 Augment with frequency wash\n\n4. <font color=green>Hardware Optimization</font>\n   \u22b2 Use Intel's NN library for correlation\n   \u22b2 Batch forward-passes via vectorization\n   \u22b2 Coarse-feather interpolation\n\n<h4>Compute Budget and Robustness</h4>\nTime estimates:\n\u208b Grid 128: 480 seconds\n\u208b Grid 256: 450 seconds\n\u208b Grid 300: 170 seconds\n\u208b Total: ~1100 seconds\n\nFallback handling:\n\u208b Use linf regression if kernel breaks\n\u208b Maintain parent solution in reductive form\n\u208b Bounce uniform grid for major grid shifts\n\n<h4>Validation and Interface</h4>\n Must ensure:\n1. <font color=purple>Feasibility</font>\n   \u22b2 Project after every evaluation\n   \u22b2 Verify sin\u2212cos normalization\n   \u22b2 Check sum against n/2\n\n2. <font color=purple>Convergence</font>\n   \u22b2 Early exit if improvement fails\n   \u22b2 Restart patterns with random seeds\n   \u22b2 Time-based grid contraction\n\n3. <font color=purple>Output</font>\n   \u22b2 Return tuple (h_values, c5, n)\n   \u22b2 Compute overlap via base correlation\n   \u22b2 Timestamp every decision\n\nWe recommend testing these strategies in batch mode for better autotuning.",
